From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Cormier Roussel <chadcormierroussel@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:59:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] airconv, d3d12: 64-bit buffer atomics through a lock table.

Shader Model 6.6's 64-bit atomics (ops 78 and 79 with the i64 overload) on
raw, structured, root-descriptor and heap-indexed buffers. Metal's own 64-bit
atomics are never emitted: on Apple GPUs they fail at pipeline creation or
crash the compiler service, and M1 has none. Instead each operation runs
under a u32 lock from a per-device table, its two halves read and written as
relaxed 32-bit atomics inside seq_cst device fences.

- airconv: kAtomic64Locks (1 << 16). InitializeDXIL sets
  ShaderInfo::use_atomic64_locks for an i64 op 78/79; setup_binding_rootsig
  then declares the root-argument field atomic64_locks last (after the heap
  fields when the root signature has flags 0xC00, declared then even without
  heap indexing), and BindingMap::GetAtomic64Locks loads it. Every other
  shader's output is unchanged.
- Lowering::UnderLock: phase 1, one cmpxchg per lane, winners run the
  critical section with no loop; phase 2, while simd_any(pending), the first
  pending lane (air.simd_is_first, behind a branch) spins, runs the section
  and releases. No spin cap.
- Lowering::Atomic64: the element pointer as the i32 path forms it, the key
  ((a >> 3) ^ (a >> 19)) & 0xFFFF of its 32-bit address, so every view of the
  same bytes takes the same lock. A pixel shader's helper lanes and an
  out-of-bounds element skip it and read 0. Typed buffers and textures stay
  refused by name.
- d3d12: MTLD3D12Device::Atomic64Locks (256 KiB, shared storage, zeroed,
  registered like the null buffers, created on first use) and
  ResetAtomic64Locks, called after a command buffer fault, which could leave
  a lock held. Every root signature gets one trailing qword, LockQwordOffset,
  which SetCompute/GraphicsRootSignature always write; the staging arrays
  grow to 67.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
